        "2": {
            "name": "text_block",
            "id": 311,
            "position_x": 862,
            "position_y": 45,
            "width": 768,
            "height": 1016,
            "visibility": "show_all",
            "draggable": true,
            "presentation_state": "show_all",
            "properties": {
                "0": {
                    "name": "block",
                    "value": "Blurs an array: replaces every value with a weighted average of its neighbourhood, the weights falling off in a bell curve (a Gaussian) with distance. It works on lists, images, grids and volumes alike, along every axis at once.\n\nUse it to soften an occupancy or heat map so its shapes stand out from the specks, to smooth a recorded curve without lag, to spread single points into soft glows, or to blur an image. sigma sets how far the blur reaches.\n\nSYNTAX:\nscipy.gaussian_filter <sigma ...>\nDefault: sigma 1.\n\nEXAMPLES:\nscipy.gaussian_filter 3\nscipy.gaussian_filter 0 2\n\nINPUTS and PARAMETERS:\n\nin:\nThe array (a list, image or grid). Every input is blurred and sent.\n\nsigma (text box, also an inlet):\nHow far the blur reaches, in samples or cells: the width of the bell curve. One number for every axis, or one per axis - 0 leaves that axis alone, so 0 2 blurs a buffer of frames x joints along the joints only, and 2 0 along time only.\n\nedges:\nAn option, under the node: what lies beyond the edges. reflect (the default) mirrors the array; nearest repeats the edge values; wrap continues from the other side;\nconstant uses 0.\n\nOUTPUTS:\n\nfiltered:\nThe blurred array, the same shape.\n\nREADING THE DEMO:\nRandom values (left), blurred with sigma 2 (right): the speckle merges into soft clouds. Blurring also averages away contrast, so the right-hand picture is shown over a narrower range. A new picture comes every two seconds.\n\nRELATED:\nscipy.median_filter removes spikes while keeping edges sharp. np.convolve smooths with weights you choose. k.gaussian_blur and tv.gaussian_blur blur images as tensors. scipy.filtfilt smooths a recording by frequency.",
                    "value_type": "str"
                },
                "1": {
                    "name": "lock",
                    "value": true,
                    "value_type": "bool"
                },
                "2": {
                    "name": "width",
                    "value": 760,
                    "value_type": "int"
                },
                "3": {
                    "name": "height",
                    "value": 1000,
                    "value_type": "int"
                },
                "4": {
                    "name": "text_size",
                    "value": "30",
                    "value_type": "str"
                }
            },
            "text": "Blurs an array: replaces every value with a weighted average of its neighbourhood, the weights falling off in a bell curve (a Gaussian) with distance. It works on lists, images, grids and volumes alike, along every axis at once.\n\nUse it to soften an occupancy or heat map so its shapes stand out from the specks, to smooth a recorded curve without lag, to spread single points into soft glows, or to blur an image. sigma sets how far the blur reaches.\n\nSYNTAX:\nscipy.gaussian_filter <sigma ...>\nDefault: sigma 1.\n\nEXAMPLES:\nscipy.gaussian_filter 3\nscipy.gaussian_filter 0 2\n\nINPUTS and PARAMETERS:\n\nin:\nThe array (a list, image or grid). Every input is blurred and sent.\n\nsigma (text box, also an inlet):\nHow far the blur reaches, in samples or cells: the width of the bell curve. One number for every axis, or one per axis - 0 leaves that axis alone, so 0 2 blurs a buffer of frames x joints along the joints only, and 2 0 along time only.\n\nedges:\nAn option, under the node: what lies beyond the edges. reflect (the default) mirrors the array; nearest repeats the edge values; wrap continues from the other side;\nconstant uses 0.\n\nOUTPUTS:\n\nfiltered:\nThe blurred array, the same shape.\n\nREADING THE DEMO:\nRandom values (left), blurred with sigma 2 (right): the speckle merges into soft clouds. Blurring also averages away contrast, so the right-hand picture is shown over a narrower range. A new picture comes every two seconds.\n\nRELATED:\nscipy.median_filter removes spikes while keeping edges sharp. np.convolve smooths with weights you choose. k.gaussian_blur and tv.gaussian_blur blur images as tensors. scipy.filtfilt smooths a recording by frequency."
        },
